  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "According to the Chinese remainder theorem, which of the following systems of congruences has a unique solution modulo $210?$ \n\n$$\n\\text{I.}\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡2 & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ x≡4 & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ x≡5 & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{II.}\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡7 & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ x≡13 & (\\text{mod}\\,21)\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{III.}\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡9 & (\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\ x≡11 & (\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "The Chinese remainder theorem states that if the natural numbers ${n}_{1},{n}_{2},…,{n}_{k}$ are pairwise coprime, meaning that $\\text{gcd}({n}_{i},{n}_{j})=1$ whenever $i\\ne j,$ then for any integers ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{k}$ the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡{a}_{1} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{1}) \\\\ x≡{a}_{2} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{2}) \\\\ \\,⋮ \\\\ x≡{a}_{k} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{k})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhas a unique solution modulo ${n}_{1}{n}_{2}⋯{n}_{k}.$\n\nLet's now inspect each of the given systems in turn.\n\n- The theorem applies to system I. The numbers $5,$ $6,$ and $7$ are pairwise coprime natural numbers. Therefore, according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo $5\\cdot 6\\cdot 7=210.$\n\n- The theorem applies to system II. The numbers $10$ and $21$ are pairwise coprime natural numbers. Therefore, according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo $10\\cdot 21=210.$\n\n- The theorem applies to system III. The numbers $14$ and $15$ are pairwise coprime natural numbers. Therefore, according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo $14\\cdot 15=210.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
